cas - the U8000 assembler of WEGA (/bin/cas), reconstructed from the binary
===========================================================================

State: all modules are done, see MODULES (text range of each module in /bin/cas and its state): ifile, nodes, instr, scanner, main, done, parser, symbols, ssymtab, locntr, expr, p2loop, ofile, gen, assemble, getops, error, jpr, jpr2, optim, listing, pst, subr and the data modules table1, table2, pstab, stable, itable, ntable. `make` builds the program (see the Makefile: `sld -i -s -e start -z -Mt4 -Md5 -Mb5 /lib/scrt0.o <modules> -Md7 stable.o -Md8 itable.o -Md9 ntable.o -Md5 /lib/slibc.a`). The facts about the binary are in host/KNOWLEDGE.md (section cas).

Two checks, different in weight
-------------------------------
`check.sh` (below) compares the code of a module with the binary. It does NOT prove the behaviour: it did not show a swapped pair of case labels, undeclared functions that return a long or a pointer (the result was cut to an int), a long argument that was passed as an int, or a forgotten assignment. These were found by running the program against the original: see test/ and host/KNOWLEDGE.md ("Test of the complete cas"). The behaviour is what counts; check.sh helps to find the place when a test fails.

How a module is checked
-----------------------
`sh check.sh WEGA=<file tree>` compiles the module with the original scc under z8k-emu and runs
host/tools/cmpmod.py: it reduces the object and the range of /bin/cas to a normal form (addresses,
labels, frame sizes and scratch registers are masked; constants, calls and the stack slots of the local
variables are kept) and lists every function as identical or differing. `-v` shows the differences.

The binary was built by an OLDER scc than the one in the system (which is why byte identity is not the aim
here; the aim is the same code, then the same behaviour). The flags were `scc -O -c` (the parser: `scc -c`);
with `-O` the shipped scc gives the closest result, so the Makefile and check.sh use it. The differences that
always show up and are not differences of the source are described in host/KNOWLEDGE.md ("Compiler revisions",
D1 ... D13); the table "Compiler differences" in src/TODO.md says which module shows which. cmpmod.py masks
frame sizes and scratch registers and converts the prologue behind the body (no `-O`) back to the front form.

The names of functions and variables are ours (the binary is stripped); cas.h declares the node layout.

parser.y
--------
`parser.y` is made into `parser.c` by the original yacc (`yacc -d parser.y`, `y.tab.c` renamed); `y.tab.h` gives
scanner.c its `YYSTYPE`. The module is compiled WITHOUT -O (with -O the shipped scc would not put the prologue
behind the body, as the binary has it). `check.sh parser` first compares the eight yacc tables with the binary
(`host/tools/cmpyacc.py`: all identical), then the code. The functions of the modules that do not exist yet are
called by their address (`fn4368`, ...); the names follow when those modules are done (see ADDRESSES).
Data: the tables and strings are the same, but the shipped scc places the strings of a function in front of the
data that follow, the old one at the end of the module (and aligns them differently), so `cmpdata` reports
differences.

assemble.c is compiled without -O (prologues behind the bodies, as in the parser; check.sh does that); cmpmod.py pairs its functions shifted by the prologue fragments.

Tests of the complete program
-----------------------------
`test/` (see test/README): the original /bin/cas (run by z8k-emu) and the rebuilt cas assemble the same sources; messages, return code and object
must be equal. exh.py (every opcode that host/tools/z8k.py decodes, and operand patterns for the others), rel.py (relative jumps, jpr/callr, chains
of jpr at the limit), opts.py (the options -l -d -r -t -i -m -v -^), exp.py (random expressions and directives; use with care: the original does not
terminate on many wrong inputs, and the results after a second error in a statement depend on the memory layout). The corpus of compiler output:
`cc -S` / `scc -S` of the sources in src/cmd and src/lib/libc.
